{:edb/db-before-t 2806 :edb/db-after-t 2807 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536119 50 #inst "2026-10-09T15:30:27.653Z" 13194139536119 true] [17592186161842 1100 "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139536119 true] [17592186161842 1100 "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139536119 false] [17592186161842 1145 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere\n\n$$\n\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nfrom $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\big\\{A\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Ax‖^{2} &= (Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\\n&= (x^{T}A^{T})(Ax) \\\\\n&= x^{T}(A^{T}A)x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nis equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 7 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}$." 13194139536119 false] [17592186161842 1145 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 7\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\}  = \\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139536119 true] [17592186161843 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/094b6043b9cce7f3ac2a7cc32fa53785d3fbf5b490ce853474f89bb4afa8291a.png)\n\nA $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA,$ find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A?$" 13194139536119 true] [17592186161843 1100 "A $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA$, find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3131/Source/Images/ba65b415e5958ff6ec13493e6641c014.png)" 13194139536119 false] [17592186161843 1145 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1$,\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $\\mid \\mid A\\mathbf v_1 \\mid \\mid = 8 = \\sigma_1$, which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64$, and\n- $\\mid \\mid A\\mathbf v_2 \\mid \\mid = 6 = \\sigma_2$, which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36$.\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = 36\n$$\n\nof $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2$." 13194139536119 false] [17592186161843 1145 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1,$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_1|| = 8 = \\sigma_1,$ which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64,$ and\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_2|| = 6 = \\sigma_2,$ which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = 36$ of $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2.$" 13194139536119 true] [17592186161844 1100 "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4,$ $\\sigma_2=2,$ and $\\sigma_3 = 0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139536119 true] [17592186161844 1100 "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4$, $\\sigma_2=2$, and $\\sigma_3 = 0$, find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139536119 false] [17592186161844 1145 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere\n\n$$\n\\big\\{\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nfrom $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\big\\{A\\mathbf{x} \\, \\mid \\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖Ax‖^{2} &= (Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\\n&= (x^{T}A^{T})(Ax) \\\\\n&= x^{T}(A^{T}A)x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1\n$$\n\nis equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A$.\n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A$. Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4$, the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{\\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4$." 13194139536119 false] [17592186161844 1145 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4,$ the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4.$" 13194139536119 true] [17592186162029 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384449 13194139536119 true] [17592186162029 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384456 13194139536119 true] [17592186162029 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384463 13194139536119 true] [17592186162029 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384470 13194139536119 true] [17592186162030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384505 13194139536119 true] [17592186162030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384512 13194139536119 true] [17592186162030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384519 13194139536119 true] [17592186162030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384526 13194139536119 true] [17592186162031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384477 13194139536119 true] [17592186162031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384484 13194139536119 true] [17592186162031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384491 13194139536119 true] [17592186162031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186384498 13194139536119 true] [17592186162286 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536119 true] [17592186162286 1100 "A $2 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{3}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the eigenvalues of $A^{T}A$ are $5$, $3$, and $0$, find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139536119 false] [17592186162286 1100 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $5,$ $3,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139536119 true] [17592186162286 1145 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=5\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139536119 true] [17592186162287 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536119 true] [17592186162287 1100 "A $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{2}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the singular values of $A$ are $σ_{1} = 10$ and $σ_{2} = 2\\sqrt{5}$, find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139536119 false] [17592186162287 1100 "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139536119 true] [17592186162287 1145 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={10}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={10}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$" 13194139536119 true] [17592186162289 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536119 true] [17592186162289 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/f26e876d5234313926a2cbaa8938e32c8a725cf3435ce3113361bfa3dc1b7997.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" 13194139536119 true] [17592186162289 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3131/Source/Images/q-139421.png)\n\nA $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{2}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^{T}A$, which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of $A^{T}A$?" 13194139536119 false] [17592186162289 1145 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={7}^{2}=49,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={5}^{2}=25.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=25$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$" 13194139536119 true] [17592186384449 1017 #edb/ref 17592186384450 13194139536119 true] [17592186384449 1089 "q-139492" 13194139536119 true] [17592186384449 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536119 true] [17592186384449 1100 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." 13194139536119 true] [17592186384449 1113 #uuid "1d8fbb39-d61e-55b7-943c-0294b7ca4f31" 13194139536119 true] [17592186384449 1145 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{ali